Here is another example that demonstrates how to extract all the links from an HTML page using Jsoup:

```java
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Element;
import org.jsoup.select.Elements;

public class LinkExtractor {
    public static void extractLinks(String html) {
        Document document = Document.parse(html);
        Elements links = document.select("a[href]");
        for (Element link : links) {
            System.out.println(link.attr("href"));
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String htmlString = "<html><body><a href='https://www.example.com'>Example</a></body></html>";
        extractLinks(htmlString);
    }
}
```

This Java program uses the Jsoup library to extract all the links from an HTML page. The `extractLinks` method takes a string of HTML and returns all the links on the page, which are stored in a list of `Element` objects.

Here is another example that demonstrates how to add JavaScript code to an HTML element using Jsoup:

```java
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Element;

public class HtmlModifier {
    public static void modifyHtml(String html) {
        Document document = Document.parse(html);
        Element scriptTag = new Element("script");
        scriptTag.text("console.log('Hello World!');");
        document.head().appendChild(scriptTag);
        System.out.println(document.toString());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String htmlString = "<html><body>Hello World!</body></html>";
        modifyHtml(htmlString);
    }
}
```

This Java program uses the Jsoup library to add a script tag with JavaScript code to an HTML element. The `modifyHtml` method takes a string of HTML and returns the modified HTML, which includes the added script tag.

